-----------------------------\n#### 3.  Shape of the left region  \n\nThe cardinalities \\(|L_r|=\\pi(r)-1\\) are a permutation of the numbers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \nRe‑order the rows so that \\(|L_{r_1}|\\ge |L_{r_2}|\\ge\\cdots\\ge |L_{r_n}|\\).  \nAfter this re‑ordering the row lengths become  \n\n\\[\nn-1,\\; n-2,\\; \\dots ,\\; 1,\\;0 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L\\) is exactly the Ferrers diagram (staircase) whose \\(i\\)-th row (from the top) contains the first \\(n-i\\) columns.  \nThe right region \\(R\\) is the mirror image of this staircase.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for tiling a staircase  \n\n**Lemma.** In a Ferrers diagram whose row lengths are strictly decreasing, any axis‑aligned rectangle contained in the diagram has width \\(1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a rectangle \\(Q\\) of width at least \\(2\\) lies inside the diagram. Let its top row be row \\(p\\) and its bottom row be row \\(q\\) (\\(p<q\\)). Because the row lengths decrease by at least one when moving from row \\(p\\) to row \\(p+1\\), the cell in column “left‑most column of \\(Q\\) + 1’’ of row \\(p+1\\) is **not** in the diagram, while that same column belongs to row \\(p\\). Consequently the rectangle would contain a cell that is not part of the diagram – a contradiction. ∎\n\nThe left staircase contains cells in exactly the columns \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \nSince each rectangle can occupy only a single column, at least one rectangle is needed for each of these \\(n-1\\) columns. Hence any tiling of \\(L\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles. The same argument applies to \\(R\\).\n\nTherefore any tiling of the whole board needs at least  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{left}}+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{right}}=2n-2\n\\]\n\nrectangles (tiles).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_c^{\\;L}= \\{(r,c)\\mid \\pi(r)>c\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation, the set \\(\\{r\\mid \\pi(r)>c\\}\\) consists of exactly the rows whose left part includes column \\(c\\); these rows are consecutive after the re‑ordering described in §3. Consequently \\(T_c^{\\;L}\\) is a vertical rectangle of width \\(1\\) and height \\(n-c\\). The family \\(\\{T_c^{\\;L}\\mid c=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) partitions \\(L\\).\n\nAnalogously, for each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_c^{\\;R}= \\{(r,c)\\mid \\pi(r)<c\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a vertical rectangle of width \\(1\\) and height \\(c-1\\). The rectangles \\(\\{T_c^{\\;R}\\mid c=2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) partition \\(R\\).\n\nAll these rectangles are pairwise disjoint, each lies entirely on grid lines, and together they cover every cell of the board except the uncovered squares \\(U\\).  \nThe total number of rectangles is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Minimality  \n\nWe have exhibited a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles and proved that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles. Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).\n\nWith \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion:**  \nThe smallest number of rectangular tiles that Matilda can place, while leaving exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column, is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
