d in every column exactly one unit square is left uncovered.\n\nDenote by \\(U\\) the set of uncovered unit squares.  \nBecause each row and each column contains precisely one element of \\(U\\), the set \\(U\\) is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a bijection \\(\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\pi(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound for the number of tiles  \n\nConsider a fixed row \\(i\\).  \nSince the cell \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) is uncovered, any tile that lies in row \\(i\\) must be completely to the left of column \\(\\pi(i)\\) or completely to the right of column \\(\\pi(i)\\).  \nConsequently **at least two** tiles are required to cover the cells of row \\(i\\) unless a tile also uses a second row.\n\nAssume a tile uses two distinct rows \\(r\\) and \\(s\\).  \nBecause the tile cannot contain the uncovered cells of those rows, the two uncovered cells must be in the *same column*; otherwise the rectangle would intersect one of them.  \nThus a tile can join at most **one** pair of rows, and any such pair must have the same column for their uncovered squares.\n\nHence at most one pair of rows can be covered by a single tile; all remaining \\(n-2\\) rows need at least two tiles each.  \nTherefore the total number of tiles \\(T\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\nT\\ge 2\\,(n-2)+1 = 2n-3 .\n\\]\n\nThe “+1’’ comes from the tile that joins the two special rows; the remaining rows each need two tiles.  \nA slightly sharper observation is that the joining tile itself occupies two rows, so we actually *save* exactly two tiles compared with the naïve count \\(2n\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{T\\ge 2n-2 } .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\) this gives \\(T\\ge 4048\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nDefine the permutation \\(\\pi\\) by  \n\n\\[\n\\pi(1)=2,\\qquad \\