ows 2-4 partially (covers column1). Row3 left part also includes column2, row4 left part includes columns2-3. Could we cover columns2-3 across rows 3-4? Intersection of left parts of rows 3 and 4 is columns1-2 (since row3 left part columns1-2, row4 left part columns1-3). Intersection of columns2-3 across rows 3-4 is column2 only (since row3 left part includes column2, row4 left part includes column2). So we could have tile covering rows 3-4, column2 (2x1) covering (3,2),(4,2). Then left part column3 of row4 remains (4,3). Could be covered by tile covering row4 column3 (1x1). So left side could be covered by 4 tiles (col1 tall, col2 tall, col3 single). Right side: row1 columns2-4, row2 columns3-4, row3 column4. Intersection of right parts: row1 right part columns2-4, row2 right part columns3-4, row3 right part column4. Intersection across rows 1-3 is column4 only. So we could have tile covering rows1-3, column4 (3x1) covering (1,4),(2,4),(3,4). Then row1 right part columns2-3 remain (1,2),(1,3). Row2 right part column3 remains (2,3). Could we cover (1,2),(1,3),(2,3) with a rectangle? Possibly tile covering rows1-2, columns2-3 would include (2,2) which is uncovered (since (2,2) is uncovered). So not allowed. So we need separate tiles: maybe tile covering row1 columns2-3 (1x2) covering (1,2),(1,3). Tile covering (2,3) single. So right side uses 3 tiles: tall column4, row1 tile covering (1,2)-(1,3), single (2,3). So total tiles = left side 4 + right side 3 = 7. That's more than 6. But maybe we can do better by different arrangement. Let's try to cover left side with 3 tiles: maybe tile covering rows2-4, columns1-2 (3x2 rectangle) includes (2,2) uncovered? Row2 column2 is uncovered (2,2). So not allowed. So can't include column2 across row2. So left side must avoid (2,2). So left side cannot be a single tall rectangle covering rows2-4, columns1-2. So we need at least 4 left side tiles as above. Right side maybe can be done with 2 tiles? Let's see: we have right side cell