-2 rectangles, which seems plausible as minimal.\n\nBut we need to verify the lower bound argument is valid given the non-overlap condition. The argument that each row with interior uncovered cell requires at least two tiles is correct. However, we need to argue that the total number of tiles is at least sum of per-row minima, i.e., at least 2n-2. But is it possible that a single tile can serve as left tile for multiple rows? Yes, but that would still count as one tile, but each row still needs at least one left tile and one right tile. However, the left tile for row i could be the same as the left tile for row j, if the rectangle covers both rows i and j and columns left of both uncovered cells. But is that possible? Let's examine.\n\nSuppose we have rows i and j with i < j. Their uncovered columns are pi(i) and pi(j). In the solution's construction, pi(i) = i, pi(j) = j. So left side of row i is columns 1..i-1. Left side of row j is columns 1..j-1. The left side of row i is a subset of left side of row j (since i-1 < j-1). So a rectangle covering rows i..j and columns 1..i-1 would cover left side of row i and part of left side of row j (columns 1..i-1). However, row j's left side also includes column i (since i < j). So the rectangle does not cover column i for row j. So row j would still need another tile to cover column i (and possibly other columns up to j-1). So we cannot combine all left sides into a single rectangle; we need to cover each \"new\" column introduced by each row. This suggests that the minimal number of rectangles needed to cover lower triangular region is exactly n-1 (or maybe n-2?). Let's compute.\n\nConsider covering lower triangular region (excluding diagonal) with rectangles that avoid diagonal. We can think of each rectangle as covering a set of rows and a set of columns, such that for any row in the rectangle, all columns in the rectangle are less than the row's diagonal column (i.e., column index < row index). So for a rectangle coverin