angles (tiles). However, could a tile covering left part also extend to other rows, covering left side cells of multiple rows? Yes, but that doesn't reduce the number of tiles needed for that row: the tile covering left part of row i could also cover left parts of other rows, but still counts as one tile. So the lower bound argument that each row with uncovered cell not at column 1 or n requires at least two tiles is correct: at least two distinct tiles must intersect that row (one covering left side, one covering right side). But could a tile covering left side of row i also cover right side of some other row j? That's fine, but each row with uncovered cell not at ends still needs at least two distinct tiles intersecting it. However, could a tile that covers left side of row i also cover right side of row i? No, because left side and right side are separated by uncovered cell. So each such row contributes at least two distinct tiles. However, could a tile covering left side of row i also be the same tile covering left side of row j? Yes, but that doesn't reduce the count of tiles needed for row i: it still uses one tile for left side. So the lower bound is: sum over rows of minimal number of tiles intersecting each row. But a tile can intersect multiple rows; the lower bound is not simply sum of per-row minimal counts because a single tile can serve multiple rows. However, the lower bound argument used is that each row with uncovered cell not at ends requires at least two tiles, but those tiles could be the same as those used for other rows. So the lower bound of total tiles >= 2*(n-2)+2 = 2n-2 may be too high? Let's think carefully.\n\nWe need to find a lower bound on total number of tiles. The argument: there are exactly two rows where uncovered cell is at column 1 or n, requiring exactly one tile each. For the other n-2 rows, each requires at least two tiles. But the total number of tiles needed to cover all rows is at least max over rows of number of tiles? Actually, each tile can intersect multiple rows, so we cannot simply sum per row. For example, suppose we have a tile that is a rectangle covering many rows and many columns. It will intersect many rows, providing coverage for each of those rows. So the per-row lower bound is not additive.\n\nBut the argument in the solution states: \"Thus there are precisely two rows that need only one tile, and the remaining n-2 rows need at least two tiles each. Therefore the total number T of tiles satisfies T ≥ 2(n-2)+1+1 = 2n-2.\" This is a mistake: they treat the per-row requirement as additive, ignoring that a single tile can serve multiple rows. However, does the condition that each row's left and right parts must be covered by distinct tiles imply that each row's left part and right part must be covered by distinct tiles that cannot be shared across rows? Let's examine.\n\nSuppose we have row i with uncovered cell at column k (2 ≤ k ≤ n-1). The left part of row i consists of columns 1..k-1. The right part consists of columns k+1..n. A tile covering left part of row i must be a rectangle that includes some set of rows (maybe multiple rows) and columns 1..k-1 (or a subset). Similarly, a tile covering right part must include columns k+1..n. Could the same tile cover left part of row i and right part of some other row j? Yes. But could a tile cover left part of row i and right part of row i? No. So each row i with interior uncovered cell must intersect at least two distinct tiles. However, those two tiles could be the same as the two tiles intersecting other rows. For example, suppose we have a tile covering all rows and columns 1..k-1 (the left side region). That tile would intersect all rows that have uncovered cells not at column 1 (i.e., all rows except possibly those with uncovered at column 1). Similarly, a tile covering all rows and columns k+1..n would intersect all rows that have uncovered cells not at column n. But note that the left side region for each row may have different widths: for row i with uncovered at column i (if we choose diagonal), left side region is columns 1..i-1, right side region is columns i+1..n. So a single tile cannot cover left side of all rows because left side region differs per row. However, we could have multiple tiles that cover overlapping sets of rows.\n\nThe solution's construction uses exactly one tile per row for left side and one per row for right side (except for first and last row). That yields 2n-2 tiles. But maybe we can reduce number of tiles by using larger rectangles that cover left parts of multiple rows simultaneously. For example, consider the diagonal permutation pi(i)=i. The left side of row i is columns 1..i-1. This region can be covered by a single tile that covers rows 2..n and columns 1..(i-1) for each i? But the left side region for row i is nested: for i=2, left side is column 1; for i=3, left side is columns 1..2; for i=4, left side is columns 1..3, etc. So we could cover all left side cells with a single tile that covers rows 2..n and columns 1..(n-1)? But that tile would also include the uncovered cells on the diagonal (i,i) for i>1? Let's check: The left side cells are those (i,j) where j < i (since uncovered at (i,i)). So the set of left side cells is the set of cells strictly below the diagonal? Actually, left side cells are those in row i, columns 1..i-1. So they are in the region below the diagonal? No, they are in the region left of the diagonal, which includes cells (i,j) with j < i. That's the region below the diagonal? In a matrix, rows increase downwards, columns increase rightwards. So j < i means column index less than row index. This region is below the diagonal? Actually, the diagonal entries are (i,i). The region where column index < row index is below the diagonal (strictly lower triangular). Similarly, right side cells are those where column index > row index, i.e., above the diagonal (strictly upper triangular). So left side cells are lower triangular region