                            "text": "We need to evaluate the solution provided for the problem: \"Consider a 2025×2025 grid of unit squares. Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of difference sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile. Determine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\"\n\nWe have a solution that claims the answer is 2n-2 = 4048.\n\nWe need to apply the hierarchical decision tree.\n\nFirst, we need to parse the problem: It's an optimization problem (minimum number of tiles). So it's a MINIMIZATION problem. According to the hierarchical decision tree, for optimization problems, at Level 1 we do not check correctness of answer (since we don't have ground truth). We only check for arithmetic errors or constraint violations. Then we go to Level 1.5 to check optimality.\n\nThus we need to see if the solution's answer is plausible, if there are any arithmetic errors, constraint violations, and then test optimality.\n\nWe need to check the solution's reasoning: It gives a lower bound of 2n-2 and a construction achieving that bound. So it claims optimality.\n\nWe need to check if the lower bound reasoning is valid. Also if the construction indeed satisfies the constraints and uses exactly 2n-2 tiles.\n\nWe need to check for any errors in the reasoning.\n\nWe need to check if the solution's construction indeed covers all squares except exactly one per row and column, and uses non-overlapping tiles.\n\nWe need to see if any hidden assumptions or mistakes.\n\nWe need to test small cases: For n=3 or n=4, does the construction work? The solution's construction uses the permutation where pi(i) = i for all i, with pi(1)=1 and pi(n)=n. Actually they set pi(i)=i for all i (including 1 and n). So uncovered squares are the diagonal squares (i,i) for all i. Then they place tiles: For row 1, tile covering cells (1,c) for c=2..n. For row n, tile covering cells (n,c) for c=1..n-1. For each middle row i (2 <= i <= n-1), they place two tiles: left part covering columns 1..i-1, and right part covering columns i+1..n. So each row i has uncovered cell at column i, and the remaining cells are covered by tiles that are contiguous segments within that row. Since each tile is a rectangle that occupies a single row (height 1), they are all 1×(some length) rectangles (i.e., horizontal strips). They are axis-aligned and disjoint because they occupy distinct rows. So they satisfy the constraints: each tile is a rectangle (height 1, width something). No overlapping because each tile is in a distinct row. Each row has exactly one uncovered cell (the diagonal). Each column also has exactly one uncovered cell (the diagonal). So the construction works.\n\nNow the lower bound: The solution argues that each row with uncovered cell not at column 1 or n must have at least two tiles, because the uncovered cell splits the row into left and right parts, each non-empty, and each part must be covered by a distinct tile because a tile cannot cross the uncovered cell (since the uncovered cell is not covered). Actually a tile could be placed that covers cells on both sides of the uncovered cell if it doesn't cover the uncovered cell? Wait, a tile is a rectangle; if it covers cells on both sides of the uncovered cell, then the rectangle would include the column of the uncovered cell, but the uncovered cell is not covered. However, a rectangle covering cells on both sides would also cover the cell at that column (the uncovered cell) unless the rectangle has a gap. But a rectangle cannot have a hole; it's contiguous. So any rectangle covering cells on both sides of column i must also cover column i, which is the uncovered cell. Since that cell must remain uncovered, the rectangle cannot include it. So the row must be split into at least two rectangles (tiles). So at least two tiles per row unless the uncovered cell is at an extreme column (1 or n), where the row is not split.\n\nThus there are exactly two rows where uncovered cell is at column 1 or n (since the permutation is bijection, each column appears exactly once). So at least 2 rows need only one tile, the rest need at least two. So lower bound is 2*(n-2) + 2 = 2n-2.\n\nThus the lower bound is valid.\n\nThus the construction matches the lower bound, giving optimality.\n\nThus the answer is correct and optimal.\n\nNow we need to check if there are any errors in the reasoning: The lower bound argument uses the fact that each row with uncovered cell not at column 1 or n must have at least two tiles. That seems valid. However, we need to consider if there might be a possibility to use a tile that covers cells in multiple rows (i.e., vertical tiles) to reduce the number of tiles. The solution's construction uses only horizontal tiles (height 1). Could we use vertical tiles that span multiple rows to reduce the total number? The lower bound argument only considered rows individually, but perhaps a tile could cover cells in multiple rows, thereby reducing the total number of tiles needed. Let's examine that.\n\nThe problem states that each tile is a rectangle, axis-aligned, covering unit squares, and each unit square is covered by at most one tile. The uncovered squares are a permutation matrix. The lower bound argument says: \"If pi(i) != 1, then cells left of uncovered cell must be covered by tiles that are completely left of column pi(i). Similarly for right side.\" It then says \"If pi(i) != 1,n the row is split into two non-empty parts, each of which needs a distinct tile; thus at least two tiles are required for that row.\" However, this argument assumes that each tile covering cells in that row cannot also cover cells in other rows. But a tile could be vertical, covering a column segment that includes cells in multiple rows. But if a tile is vertical, it would cover cells in the same column across multiple rows. However, the row i's left part consists of cells (i, c) for c pi(i). A vertical tile covering column c would also cover cells (i', c) for other rows i'. But that tile would also need to avoid covering any uncovered cell (i', pi(i')). If pi(i') = c for some other row i', then that cell is uncovered, so the tile cannot include column c at row i'. But if pi(i') != c, then the tile could include that column. So a vertical tile could cover cells in the left part of row i and also cells in other rows' left parts, possibly reducing tile count.\n\nThus the lower bound argument that each row with pi(i) not at extremes requires at least two tiles may be too simplistic: It assumes each tile is confined to a single row, but tiles could be larger rectangles covering multiple rows.\n\nWe need to examine if the lower bound can be improved: Could we cover the left part of multiple rows with a single vertical tile? Let's consider the uncovered squares are a permutation matrix. The left part of row i is columns 1..pi(i)-1. The right part is columns pi(i)+1..n. The uncovered squares are exactly one per column as well. So each column j has exactly one uncovered square at row pi^{-1}(j). So column j's uncovered cell is at row i where pi(i) = j. So column j's uncovered cell is at row i = pi^{-1}(j). So the column j is split into two parts: rows above the uncovered cell and rows below the uncovered cell.\n\nThus we could consider covering the left part of row i using vertical tiles that cover column c for c pi(i). But each column c has an uncovered cell at some row r = pi^{-1}(c). So column c is split into two parts: rows above r and rows below r. So any vertical tile covering column c cannot cross the uncovered cell at row r. So a vertical tile can only cover either rows above r or rows below r in column c. So the left part of row i (cells (i,c) with c pi(i)) can be covered by vertical tiles that are either above or below the uncovered cell in column c. But if we try to cover cell (i,c) with a vertical tile that also covers other rows, we must ensure that the tile does not cross the uncovered cell in column c. So we could have a vertical tile covering a contiguous block of rows in column c that does not include the uncovered cell row. So we could potentially cover many left cells across rows with a single vertical tile per column segment.\n\nThus the lower bound argument that each row needs at least two tiles may not hold if we can use vertical tiles to cover multiple rows' left parts (or right parts) simultaneously. However, the lower bound argument says \"if pi(i) != 1,n the row is split into two non-empty parts, each of which needs a distinct tile\". This is true if we restrict to tiles that are axis-aligned rectangles that cannot cross the uncovered cell. But a tile could be a rectangle that includes cells on both sides of the uncovered cell but not the uncovered cell itself, if the rectangle has a \"hole\"? No, a rectangle cannot have a hole. So any rectangle covering cells on both sides of column pi(i) must include the column pi(i) at row i, which is the uncovered cell. So it cannot cross the uncovered cell horizontally. So the row must be covered by at least two distinct tiles, but those tiles could be vertical rectangles that extend across multiple rows, but they still must be distinct tiles for each side of the uncovered cell. So each row's left part must be covered by some tile(s). It could be covered by a vertical tile that also covers other rows' left parts. But that still counts as a tile (the same tile covering multiple rows). So the row i's left part is covered by at least one tile (maybe a vertical tile covering many rows). Similarly, the right part is covered by at least one tile. So each row with pi(i) not at extremes contributes at least 2 \"tile sides\"? Actually each row contributes at least two \"tiles\" but those tiles could be shared across rows. Wait, if a vertical tile covers left parts of multiple rows, then that tile counts as one tile for each of those rows. So the total number of tiles needed may be less than 2*(n-2)+2. Because we could reuse tiles across rows.\n\nBut the lower bound argument says \"at least two tiles are required for that row\". That is true in the sense that the row's left part and right part cannot be covered by the same tile, but it doesn't guarantee that those tiles are distinct across rows. So the lower bound of 2n-2 is based on counting per row, not per tile. However, we can try to improve the lower bound by counting something else, maybe counting total number of \"segments\" needed across rows and columns.\n\nWe need to examine if the lower bound argument is indeed valid for the minimal number of tiles, or if there is a possibility to use fewer than 2n-2 tiles by using vertical tiles that cover multiple rows.\n\nLet's test small n to see if we can achieve fewer than 2n-2 tiles. For n=3, 2n-2 = 4. The solution's construction uses 4 tiles: row 1 tile covering columns 2-3 (1 tile), row 3 tile covering columns 1-2 (1 tile), row 2 left tile covering column 1 (1 tile), row 2 right tile covering column 3 (1 tile). That's 4 tiles. Could we do better? Let's try to find a placement with 3 tiles for n=3. We need each row and column to have exactly one uncovered cell. So we need a permutation matrix of size 3. Let's say uncovered squares are (1,1), (2,2), (3,3). We need to cover the other 6 squares with at most 3 tiles. Each tile is a rectangle. Could we cover all 6 squares with 3 rectangles? Possibly. For instance, tile covering (1,2)-(1,3) (row 1, columns 2-3) is one tile. Tile covering (2,1)-(2,1) is a 1x1 tile (just cell (2,1)). Tile covering (2,3)-(3,3) is a rectangle covering cells (2,3) and (3,3)? Wait (3,3) is uncovered, cannot be covered. So not that. Let's think: maybe we can use a vertical tile covering column 2 rows 1 and 2 (cells (1,2) and (2,2)), but (2,2) is uncovered, so cannot. So vertical tile cannot cross uncovered cells. So each column also has an uncovered cell. So any vertical tile covering column j must avoid the row where the uncovered cell lies. So a vertical tile can cover either above or below that row. For n=3, each column has an uncovered cell at row equal to column (if using diagonal). So column 1 has uncovered at row 1, column 2 at row 2, column 3 at row 3. So vertical tile can cover column 1 rows 2-3 (cells (2,1) and (3,1)). That's a vertical tile covering two cells. Similarly, column 2 can cover rows 1 and 3? No, cannot cross row 2. So can cover rows 1 only (cell (1,2)) or rows 3 only (cell (3,2)). Column 3 can cover rows 1-2 (cells (1,3) and (2,3)). So we could cover (2,1) and (3,1) with one vertical tile, (1,3) and (2,3) with another vertical tile, and (1,2) with a third tile. That's 3 tiles covering all 6 cells: tile 1: column 1 rows 2-3 (vertical), tile 2: column 3 rows 1-2 (vertical), tile 3: cell (1,2) (1x1 tile). Does this satisfy constraints? Let's check each row: Row 1 has uncovered at (1,1). Row 1 also has cells (1,2) covered by tile 3, and (1,3) covered by tile 2. So row 1 has uncovered cell (1,1) only. Row 2 has uncovered at (2,2). Row 2 also has cell (2,1) covered by tile 1, and cell (2,3) covered by tile 2. Row 3 has uncovered at (3,3). Row 3 also has cell (3,1) covered by tile 1, and cell (3,2) is uncovered? Wait (3,2) is not uncovered; the uncovered cell in row 3 is (3,3). So (3,2) is not covered by any tile in this arrangement. Did we cover (3,2)? No. So we need to cover (3,2) as well. So the arrangement fails to cover (3,2). Could we adjust? Let's try a different permutation: maybe uncovered squares are not diagonal. Let's try uncovered squares at (1,2), (2,3), (3,1). That's a permutation (1->2, 2->3, 3->1). Then columns: column 1 uncovered at row 3, column 2 uncovered at row 1, column 3 uncovered at row 2. Let's see if we can cover all other cells with 3 tiles. The uncovered cells are (1,2), (2,3), (3,1). The remaining cells are:\n\nRow 1: (1,1), (1,3)\nRow 2: (2,1), (2,2)\nRow 3: (3,2), (3,3)\n\nWe need to cover these 6 cells with 3 rectangles. Could we use vertical tiles:\n\n- Column 1: uncovered at row 3, so column 1 can be covered by rows 1-2 (cells (1,1), (2,1)). That's a vertical tile covering (1,1) and (2,1).\n- Column 2: uncovered at row 1, so column 2 can be covered by rows 2-3 (cells (2,2), (3,2)). That's a vertical tile covering (2,2) and (3,2).\n- Column 3: uncovered at row 2, so column 3 can be covered by rows 1 and 3 (cells (1,3), (3,3)). However, they are not contiguous (row 1 and row 3 with row 2 missing). A rectangle covering column 3 rows 1-3 would include (2,3) which is uncovered, so not allowed. So we cannot cover both (1,3) and (3,3) with a single vertical tile because the uncovered cell at (2,3) blocks. So we need separate tiles for (1,3) and (3,3). That would give total 4 tiles: vertical tile for column 1 (2 cells), vertical tile for column 2 (2 cells), tile for (1,3), tile for (3,3). That's 4 tiles, not 3.\n\nMaybe we can use horizontal tiles: Row 1: cells (1,1) and (1,3) are not contiguous (col 2 is uncovered). So cannot be covered by a single horizontal tile. So need separate tiles for each. So seems 4 tiles is minimal for n=3? Let's try to find a placement with 3 tiles in any permutation.\n\nWe can try to use a 2x2 tile covering a block of four cells, but must avoid uncovered cells. For n=3, any 2x2 block will include at least one uncovered cell? Let's examine possibilities. Uncovered cells are one per row and column. So they form a permutation matrix. The complement (the covered cells) is the rest. Could there be a 2x2 rectangle that does not intersect any uncovered cell? For a 2x2 rectangle to avoid uncovered cells, it must be placed in rows i,i+1 and columns j,j+1 such that none of the four cells are uncovered. That means the permutation does not have any of those cells as (i, pi(i)) or (i+1, pi(i+1)). So we need pi(i) not equal to j or j+1, and pi(i+1) not equal to j or j+1. Is that possible? Yes, for some permutations. For example, for n=3, we cannot have a 2x2 rectangle because the grid is only 3x3, and any 2x2 rectangle will involve two rows and two columns. Since there are only three rows and columns, any 2x2 rectangle will be either rows 1-2 and columns 1-2, rows 1-2 and columns 2-3, rows 2-3 and columns 1-2, rows 2-3 and columns 2-3. Let's test each with a permutation. For permutation (1->2,2->3,3->1), the uncovered cells are (1,2),(2,3),(3,1). The rectangle rows 1-2, columns 1-2 includes cells (1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2). It includes (1,2) which is uncovered, so not allowed. Rows 1-2, columns 2-3 includes (1,2),(1,3),(2,2),(2,3) includes (1,2) and (2,3) uncovered. Rows 2-3, columns 1-2 includes (2,1),(2,2),(3,1),(3,2) includes (3,1) uncovered. Rows 2-3, columns 2-3 includes (2,2),(2,3),(3,2),(3,3) includes (2,3) uncovered. So no 2x2 rectangle works. So any tile covering more than 2 cells must be a 1x2 or 2x1 rectangle (horizontal or vertical). So the best we can do is cover pairs of cells with vertical/horizontal tiles. The uncovered cells are arranged such that each column and each row has exactly one uncovered cell. So we can pair cells in the same column above or below the uncovered cell, or in the same row left or right of the uncovered cell. The maximum pairing possible is to pair each column's cells above the uncovered cell into a vertical tile, and each column's cells below the uncovered cell into another vertical tile. But each column's uncovered cell splits the column into top and bottom parts. For n=3, each column has exactly one uncovered cell, leaving either 0,1, or 2 cells to cover. For column 1 (uncovered at row 3), there are rows 1-2 above; we can cover them with a vertical tile. For column 2 (uncovered at row 1), there are rows 2-3 below; we can cover them with a vertical tile. For column 3 (uncovered at row 2), there is row 1 above and row 3 below; they are not contiguous, so cannot be covered by a single vertical tile; need two separate tiles. So total tiles = 1 (col1) + 1 (col2) + 2 (col3) = 4. So minimal is 4. So for n=3, 2n-2 = 4 matches minimal.\n\nNow for n=4, 2n-2 = 6. Let's test if we can achieve fewer than 6. Let's try to find a construction with 5 tiles. The solution's construction uses 6 tiles: row 1 tile covering columns 2-4 (1 tile), row 4 tile covering columns 1-3 (1 tile), rows 2 and 3 each have left and right tiles (2 each), total 6. Could we use vertical tiles to reduce? Let's attempt to find a permutation and tile arrangement that uses 5 tiles.\n\nWe need a permutation matrix of size 4. Let's consider the diagonal permutation (1->1,2->2,3->3,4->4). Then each column has uncovered at same row. The uncovered cells are (1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(4,4). The remaining cells are 12 cells. We need to cover them with as few rectangles as possible. Let's consider vertical tiles: For column 1, uncovered at row 1, so rows 2-4 below are contiguous, can be covered by a vertical tile covering (2,1),(3,1),(4,1) (size 3x1). For column 2, uncovered at row 2, rows 1 above (1,2) and rows 3-4 below (3,2),(4,2). The above part is a single cell (1,2) can be covered by a 1x1 tile or combined with something else horizontally. The below part rows 3-4 are contiguous, can be covered by a vertical tile covering (3,2),(4,2). For column 3, uncovered at row 3, rows 1-2 above (1,3),(2,3) contiguous, can be covered by vertical tile covering (1,3),(2,3). Rows 4 below (4,3) single cell. For column 4, uncovered at row 4, rows 1-3 above (1,4),(2,4),(3,4) contiguous, can be covered by vertical tile covering (1,4),(2,4),(3,4). So we have vertical tiles: col1 (rows2-4) = tile1; col2 (rows3-4) = tile2; col3 (rows1-2) = tile3; col4 (rows1-3) = tile4. That's 4 vertical tiles covering 3+2+2+3 = 10 cells. Uncovered cells left: (1,2) and (4,3) are not covered. That's 2 cells left. They could be covered by a single horizontal tile? (1,2) is in row1, (4,3) is in row4, not same row, cannot be same tile. Could we combine them into a 2x2 tile? No, they are far apart. So we need at least 2 more tiles (maybe 1x1 each). So total tiles = 4 + 2 = 6. So same as lower bound.\n\nCould we do better by using a different permutation? Let's try a permutation that might allow more vertical tiles to cover more cells. For each column, we can cover the contiguous segment above or below the uncovered cell. The uncovered cell splits each column into two contiguous segments. If we can arrange the permutation such that many columns have uncovered cells at the top or bottom, then we can cover the larger segment with a single vertical tile. However, each column must have exactly one uncovered cell, and each row must have exactly one uncovered cell. So the permutation is a bijection. The minimal number of tiles might be related to the number of \"breaks\" in the permutation.\n\nThe solution's lower bound argument uses rows, but we could also consider columns. The same reasoning yields that each column with uncovered cell not at top or bottom must have at least two tiles covering its cells (above and below). So each column not at extremes contributes at least 2 tiles. Since exactly two columns have uncovered cell at top (row 1) and bottom (row n), we get at least 2n-2 tiles. So the lower bound is symmetric: each row and each column yields the same bound. But we need to be careful: the lower bound argument for rows gave at least 2 tiles per row except two rows. The same for columns gives at least 2 tiles per column except two columns. However, the tiles counted for rows and columns are the same tiles; we cannot double count. The lower bound argument uses rows only, but we could also use columns to argue that each row's left part must be covered by at least one tile, and each row's right part must be covered by at least one tile. But those tiles could be vertical covering multiple rows. However, the column argument also says each column's top part must be covered by at least one tile, and bottom part by at least one tile. So overall we have a bipartite covering problem: we need to cover each row's left and right parts, and each column's top and bottom parts. The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover the complement of a permutation matrix is known to be 2n-2. This is a known combinatorial result: the minimum number of rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix (i.e., the board with one cell removed from each row and column) is 2n-2. This is essentially the problem of covering a chessboard with non-attacking rooks? Actually it's a known problem: the minimum number of rectangles needed to cover all cells of an n×n board except a permutation matrix is 2n-2. The solution uses the \"grid graph\" and \"rook covering\" arguments. So the answer is plausible.\n\nThus the solution's lower bound is correct. However, we need to verify that the lower bound argument is rigorous: does it guarantee that each row not at extremes requires at least two tiles, even if those tiles could be vertical covering multiple rows? The argument says \"the row is split into two non‑empty parts, each of which needs a distinct tile\". It doesn't claim that those tiles cannot be shared across rows. It just says each part needs a distinct tile (distinct from each other, but not necessarily distinct from other rows' tiles). However, the phrase \"distinct tile\" could be interpreted as \"a tile that is distinct from the other part's tile\" (i.e., the left part and right part cannot be covered by the same tile). That is correct. It does not claim that the left part's tile cannot be shared with other rows' left parts. So the lower bound argument is valid: each row contributes at least 2 \"parts\" that must be covered by at least 2 tiles (maybe shared across rows). But the counting argument \"2*(n-2)+2\" assumes each row's left part and right part require distinct tiles that are not shared across rows. Actually they sum up the number of tiles needed across rows: they count each row's left part as requiring a tile, and each row's right part as requiring a tile. But if a tile can cover left parts of multiple rows, then the total number of tiles could be less than the sum of row parts.\n\nThus the lower bound argument might be flawed if it assumes each row's left part must be covered by a distinct tile not used elsewhere. Let's examine the exact phrasing:\n\n\"Consequently\n\n* if \\(\\pi(i)\\neq 1,n\\) the row is split into two non‑empty parts, each of which needs a distinct tile; thus **at least two tiles** are required for that row;\n* if \\(\\pi(i)=1\\) (resp. \\(\\pi(i)=n\\)) the whole row lies to the right (resp. left) of the uncovered cell, so **exactly one tile** suffices for that row.\"\n\nThen they say: \"Because \\(\\pi\\) is a bijection, the column \\(1\\) occurs as \\(\\pi(i)\\) for exactly one index \\(i\\), and the column \\(n\\) occurs as \\(\\pi(j)\\) for exactly one index \\(j\\). Hence there are precisely two rows that need only one tile, and the remaining \\(n-2\\) rows need at least two tiles each. Therefore the total number \\(T\\) of tiles satisfies \\(T\\ge 2\\,(n-2)+1+1 = 2n-2\\).\"\n\nThis argument indeed assumes that each row's needed tiles are distinct across rows. They treat each row's tile count as additive. However, they didn't justify that the tiles covering different rows cannot be the same tile. But a tile cannot cover cells from two different rows unless it is vertical (height >1). But if a tile is vertical, it covers multiple rows in the same column. However, that vertical tile would be covering cells in a column that are either left or right of the uncovered cell for each row. But can a vertical tile cover left parts of multiple rows? Let's consider a column c. For each row i, the left part of row i consists of columns 1..pi(i)-1. So a vertical tile in column c can cover cell (i,c) only if c pi(i). So for any row i where pi(i) > c, the cell (i,c) is in the left part of row i. Similarly, for rows where pi(i) c, the cell (i,c) is in the right part of row i. So a vertical tile in column c can cover cells that belong to left parts of some rows and right parts of other rows. But each tile must avoid covering any uncovered cell (i, pi(i)). So column c cannot include row i where pi(i) = c. So the vertical tile can be placed in column c either above the uncovered cell (rows pi^{-1}(c)) or below (rows > pi^{-1}(c)). So vertical tile can cover left parts of rows above the uncovered cell (if pi(i) > c) and also right parts of rows above the uncovered cell (if pi(i) c). Actually for rows above the uncovered cell (i pi^{-1}(c)), the uncovered cell in column c is at row pi^{-1}(c). So rows above that are i pi^{-1}(c). For those rows, the cell (i,c) is either left or right part depending on pi(i) relative to c. So a vertical tile above the uncovered cell can cover a mixture of left and right parts across rows. Similarly for rows below.\n\nThus a vertical tile can cover cells belonging to left parts of some rows and right parts of other rows. So the counting argument that each row's left part requires a distinct tile is not necessarily true: one vertical tile could serve as the left part tile for multiple rows (if those rows have the uncovered cell to the right of column c). But the row's left part is the set of cells left of its uncovered cell; a vertical tile in column c that is left of the uncovered cell for row i (i.e., c pi(i)) can cover that cell. However, that vertical tile might also cover cells that are right of the uncovered cell for some other row j (if pi(j) c). So that vertical tile would serve as the right part tile for row j. So a single tile could serve as the left part tile for some rows and right part tile for others. So the lower bound based on rows alone might be insufficient.\n\nNevertheless, the lower bound argument also uses the fact that each column also must be split into top and bottom parts, each requiring a distinct tile. 