n-2\\) rectangles\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal).  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nA_c=\\{(r,c)\\mid r=c+1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\((n-c)\\times1\\) vertical strip covering all cells **below** the diagonal in column \\(c\\).  \n\nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nB_c=\\{(r,c)\\mid r=1,\\dots ,c-1\\},\n\\]\n\na \\((c-1)\\times1\\) vertical strip covering all cells **above** the diagonal in column \\(c\\).\n\nThe sets \\(\\{A_c\\}_{c=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{B_c\\}_{c=2}^{n}\\) are pairwise disjoint, they avoid all diagonal cells, and together they cover every cell of the board except the diagonal holes.  \nThe number of rectangles used is  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{A_c\\}\\,|+|\\,\\{B_c\\}\\,|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\le 2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Lower bound – at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles are necessary\n\nFix an arbitrary permutation \\(\\pi\\).  \nConsider a column \\(c\\) (\\(1\\le c\\le n\\)).  \nExactly one row, namely \\(r=\\pi^{-1}(c)\\), contains the uncovered square \\((r,c)\\).  \nConsequently the cells of column \\(c\\) are split into two (possibly empty) contiguous blocks:\n\n* **Upper block** \\(U_c=\\{(i,c)\\mid 1\\le i<r\\}\\);\n* **Lower block** \\(L_c=\\{(i,c)\\mid r<i\\le n\\}\\).\n\nA rectangle is an axis‑parallel product of a set of consecutive rows with a set of consecutive columns.  \nIf a rectangle contained cells from both \\(U_c\\) and \\(L_c\\), its row interval would contain the row \\(r\\); because its column interval contains \\(c\\), the rectangle would also contain the cell \\((r,c)\\), which is uncovered – a contradiction.  \nHence **no rectangle can intersect both blocks of the same column**.  \n\nThus each non‑empty block must be covered by a distinct rectangle.\n\nNow count th