\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Number the rows from top to bottom by \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) and the columns from left to right by \\(1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Structure of the uncovered squares  \n\nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\n\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the cell \\((i,\\pi(i))\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Reduction to the diagonal case  \n\nApply the same permutation \\(\\sigma\\) to the rows **and** to the columns:\nreplace row \\(i\\) by row \\(\\sigma(i)\\) and column \\(j\\) by column \\(\\sigma(j)\\).\nEvery axis\u2011parallel rectangle is still a rectangle after this relabelling,\nand the uncovered squares become the permutation matrix of  \n\n\\[\n\\pi'=\\sigma^{-1}\\!\\circ\\!\\pi\\!\\circ\\!\\sigma .\n\\]\n\nThus the minimal number of tiles needed for \\(\\pi\\) equals the minimal number\nneeded for \\(\\pi'\\).  Choosing \\(\\sigma=\\pi^{-1}\\) gives \\(\\pi'=\\text{id}\\).\nHence we may assume, without loss of generality, that  \n\n\\[\n\\pi(i)=i\\qquad\\text{for all }i,\n\\]\n\ni.e. the uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Two triangular regions  \n\nWith the diagonal holes the set of covered cells splits into  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<i\\le n\\}\\qquad\\text{(lower\u2011left triangle)},\n\\]\n\\[\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<j\\le n\\}\\qquad\\text{(upper\u2011right triangle)}.\n\\]\n\nA rectangle that contains a diagonal cell would contain an uncovered square,\nwhich is forbidden; therefore **every tile lies entirely in \\(L\\) or entirely in \\(R\\).**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  A lower bound of \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles  \n\n#### 4.1  The left triangle  \n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the cell  \n\n\\[\na_k=(k+1,k)\\in L .\n\\]\n\nThese are the cells immediately below the diagonal.  \nAssume a rectangle \\(Q\\subseteq L\\) contains two distinct cells\n\\(a_{k}\\) and \\(a_{\\ell}\\) with \\(k<\\ell\\).\nSince \\(Q\\) is axis\u2011parallel, it must contain the whole sub\u2011rectangle\nwith rows \\(k+1,\\dots ,\\ell+1\\) and columns \\(k,\\dots ,\\ell\\); in particular\nit contains the diagonal cell \\((k+1,k+1)\\), which is uncovered \u2013 a contradiction.\nHence **each rectangle can cover at most one of the cells \\(a_1,\\dots ,a_{n-1}\\).**\nAll these \\(n-1\\) cells have to be covered, so at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required for \\(L\\).\n\n#### 4.2  The right triangle  \n\nBy symmetry consider the cells  \n\n\\[\nb_k=(k,k+1)\\in R\\qquad(k=1,\\dots ,n-1).\n\\]\n\nThe same argument shows that a rectangle contained in \\(R\\) can contain at most one of the \\(b_k\\), therefore at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed for \\(R\\).\n\n#### 4.3  Total lower bound  \n\nConsequently any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2(n-1)=2\\cdot2024=4048\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nKeep the diagonal holes \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Covering the left triangle.*  \nFor each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT^{L}_{k}= \\{(i,j)\\mid k+1\\le i\\le n,\\; 1\\le j\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\nIt has dimensions \\((n-k)\\times k\\); every cell satisfies \\(j<i\\), so\n\\(T^{L}_{k}\\subseteq L\\).  \nThe family \\(\\{T^{L}_{k}\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) partitions \\(L\\): a cell\n\\((i,j)\\in L\\) belongs to the unique rectangle with \\(k=j\\).\n\n*Covering the right triangle.*  \nFor each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT^{R}_{k}= \\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\; k+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nof dimensions \\(k\\times (n-k)\\); every cell satisfies \\(i<j\\), so\n\\(T^{R}_{k}\\subseteq R\\).  \nThe family \\(\\{T^{R}_{k}\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) partitions \\(R\\).\n\nThe two families are disjoint, together they cover every unit square except the\n\\(n\\) diagonal holes, and no tile contains a hole.  \nThe total number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n|\\{T^{L}_{k}\\}|+|\\{T^{R}_{k}\\}|=(n-1)+(n-1)=4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have proved that any tiling satisfying the conditions needs at least\n\\(4048\\) tiles, and we have exhibited a tiling that uses exactly \\(4048\\) tiles.\nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
