oint tiles and covers all of \\(A\\).\n\n*Tiles covering \\(B\\).*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nC_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(C_j\\) is a \\((n-j)\\times 1\\) rectangle; it contains only cells of \\(B\\) and no diagonal cell.\nThe family \\(\\{C_j\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) disjoint tiles and covers all of \\(B\\).\n\nThe two families are disjoint (one lies strictly above, the other strictly below the diagonal) and together cover every cell of the board except the diagonal squares, which are precisely the uncovered squares required.  \nHence we have a tiling with  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound – at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary  \n\nConsider any admissible tiling (with arbitrary permutation \\(\\pi\\)).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nA_\\pi=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\pi(i)\\}\\qquad\\text{(cells to the right of the uncovered square in their row)},\n\\]\n\\[\nB_\\pi=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\pi(i)\\}\\qquad\\text{(cells to the left of the uncovered square in their row)}.\n\\]\n\nEvery covered cell belongs either to \\(A_\\pi\\) or to \\(B_\\pi\\); the two sets are disjoint.\n\n---\n\n**Claim 1.**  \nEvery tile that lies entirely inside \\(A_\\pi\\) has height 1 (it occupies a single row).  \n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a tile \\(T\\subseteq A_\\pi\\) has height at least 2, i.e. it contains two distinct rows\n\\(i_1<i_2\\). Because \\(T\\) is a rectangle, it also contains the cell \\((i_1,\\pi(i_2))\\).  \nSince \\(\\pi(i_2)\\) is the column of the uncovered square in row \\(i_2\\), we have \\(\\pi(i_2)>\\pi(i_2)\\) is false, but \\(\\pi(i_2)\\le\\pi(i_2)\\). In particular \\((i_1,\\pi(i_2))\\) lies **on or to the left** of the uncovered square of row \\(i_1\\), contradicting the fact that all cells of \\(T\\) are in \\(A_\\pi\\) (strictly to the right of the uncovered square of their rows). Hence the height must be 1. ∎\n\nConsequently each tile covering a cell of \\(A_\\pi\\) is a horizontal rectangle of the form\n\\(1\\times k\\) and occupies cells of **exactly one** row.\n\n---\n\n**Claim 2.**  \nEvery tile that lies entirely inside \\(B_\\pi\\) has width 1 (it occupies a single column).  \n\n*Proof.*  \nThe argument is symmetric to Claim 1, using columns instead of rows. ∎\n\nThus any tiling of the board consists of\n\n* horizontal tiles, each confined to a single row of \\(A_\\pi\\);\n* vertical tiles, each confined to a single column of \\(B_\\pi\\).\n\nNow count the rows that contain cells of \\(A_\\pi\\).  \nFor every row \\(i\\) the uncovered square \\((i,\\pi(i))\\) is **not** the rightmost column, because there exists at least one column \\(j>\\pi(i)\\) (otherwise the row would have no uncovered square to its right, contradicting the condition that each column also contains exactly one uncovered square). Hence each row \\(i\\) contains at least one cell of \\(A_\\pi\\).  \nSince a horizontal tile can cover cells of only one row, we need **at least one horizontal tile for each of the \\(n\\) rows**.  \n\nHowever, the row whose uncovered square lies in the last column (i.e. \\(\\pi(i)=n\\)) has no cell to its right, so that particular row contributes **zero** horizontal tiles. Hence the number of rows that actually require a horizontal tile is \\(n-1\\).\n\nAnalogously, exactly one column (the one that contains the uncovered square in the first row) has no cell to its left, so the number of columns that require a vertical tile is also \\(n-1\\).\n\nTherefore any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Minimality  \n\nThe construction of Section 1 achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and Section 2 proves that no tiling can use fewer. Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2 = 4