e can use only one column, at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are necessary to tile \\(L\\). The same argument gives at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles for \\(R\\). Hence any tiling of the whole board needs at least  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]  \n   tiles.\n\n5. **Construction attaining the bound (identity permutation).**  \n\n   Choose the permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \n   *Left region.* It consists of all cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j<i\\).  \n   For each column \\(c\\;(1\\le c\\le n-1)\\) consider the vertical rectangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_c^{L}= \\{(i,c)\\mid i=c+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n   \\]  \n\n   In the identity permutation column \\(c\\) appears exactly in rows \\(c+1,\\dots ,n\\), which are consecutive; thus \\(T_c^{L}\\) is a legitimate rectangle. The family \\(\\{T_c^{L}\\}_{c=1}^{n-1}\\) partitions the left region, using \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\n   *Right region.* It consists of all cells \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j>i\\).  \n   For each column \\(c\\;(2\\le c\\le n)\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   T_c^{R}= \\{(i,c)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,c-1\\},\n   \\]  \n\n   a vertical rectangle covering exactly the rows where column \\(c\\) lies to the right of the hole. These \\(n-1\\) rectangles partition the right region.\n\n   Altogether we have \\( (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) non\u2011overlapping rectangles covering every cell except the \\(n\\) uncovered squares.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction both give the same number, so the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Index the unit squares of the \\(n\\times n\\) board by ordered pairs \\((r,c)\\) with \\(1\\le r,c\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nEach row must contain exactly one uncovered square and each column must also contain exactly one uncovered square.  \nConsequently there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\n\\pi:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the set of uncovered squares is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(r,\\pi(r))\\mid r=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Left and right regions  \n\nFor a fixed row \\(r\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_r=\\{(r,c)\\mid 1\\le c<\\pi(r)\\},\\qquad   \nR_r=\\{(r,c)\\mid \\pi(r)<c\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nL=\\bigcup_{r=1}^{n}L_r,\\qquad   \nR=\\bigcup_{r=1}^{n}R_r .\n\\]\n\n\\(L\\) consists of all covered cells that lie **left** of the uncovered square in their row; \\(R\\) consists of all covered cells that lie **right** of the uncovered square.  \nClearly  \n\n\\[\nL\\cap R=\\varnothing ,\\qquad L\\cup R\\cup U=\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Shape of the left region  \n\nThe cardinalities \\(|L_r|=\\pi(r)-1\\) are a permutation of the numbers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \nRe\u2011order the rows so that  \n\n\\[\n|L_{r_1}|\\ge |L_{r_2}|\\ge\\cdots\\ge |L_{r_n}|.\n\\]\n\nAfter this re\u2011ordering the row lengths become  \n\n\\[\nn-1,\\;n-2,\\;\\dots ,\\;1,\\;0 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L\\) is exactly the Ferrers (staircase) diagram whose \\(k\\)-th row from the top contains the first \\(n-k\\) columns.  \nThe right region \\(R\\) is the mirror image of this staircase.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A lower bound for tiling a staircase  \n\n**Lemma.** In a Ferrers diagram whose row lengths are strictly decreasing, any axis\u2011aligned rectangle contained in the diagram has width\u202f\\(1\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(Q\\) of width \\(w\\ge2\\) lies inside the diagram.  \nLet its top row be \\(p\\) and its bottom row be \\(q\\) with \\(p<q\\).  \nBecause the row lengths drop